00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.720
So let's talk a little
bit about our products.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:27.710
Intel Xeon scalable product,
also known as Skylake,

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:31.160
is the latest generation of
Intel CPUs available in GCP

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:34.220
since first quarter of 2017.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:37.160
These processors increase
the number of CPU cores

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:42.110
from previous generation, which
translates to 50% larger VMs.

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:44.360
You can now select
Skylake-based VMs

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:49.960
with up to 96 vCPUs and memory
as large as 1.4 terabyte.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:52.960
Skylake also incorporates
many micro-architectural

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.070
advancements, including
significantly larger caches,

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:01.120
which translates to applications
being able to store larger data

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.060
structures closer to the core.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:06.790
If your data structure
doesn't fit on the core,

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:11.420
you have more memory bandwidth
to access the memory faster.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.090
And on the execution
side, Skylake

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:15.940
decreases the instruction
latencies of many instructions

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.640
and adds wider execution.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.940
At the same time, Skylake
implements the latest

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:26.660
generation of Intel vector
instructions, AVX-512,

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:31.700
which provides twice as wide as
previous generation in vector

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:38.090
sizes and can boost floating
point performance up to 82%.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:41.600
All of this translates to
faster vCPUs and containers

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.950
based on Skylake in GCE and GKE,
as well as other GCP products.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.850
As you know, one
of the essentials

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:53.820
behind convolutional
neural network modeling

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.210
is matrix multiplication.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.980
In Xeon CPUs, we're
accelerating instructions

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.020
to perform these operations
as efficiently as possible.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:05.880
Skylake today
supports FP32 vector

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:10.680
multiply and add instructions,
as well as INT8 instructions.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.380
Next generation of
Intel Xeon processors

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:16.370
offers our new
VNNI instructions.

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:19.470
VNNI stands for vector
neural network instructions.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:21.540
And includes
hardware acceleration

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.070
for INT8 convolution
instructions.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:31.530
In next generation of
Intel Xeon processors,

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.350
3-AVX instructions to
perform intake operations

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:37.920
are fused into one, providing
speedup due to consuming

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:40.020
less clock cycles.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.750
We're working very closely
with framework developers

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:44.940
to make these
instructions integrated

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:47.520
into their frameworks so
that they can take advantage

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:50.520
of the performance boost
that these instructions bring

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:51.885
to your application.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:57.921
So we talked about
our new instructions

00:09:57.921 --> 00:09:59.420
and all the goodness
in the hardware

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:03.030
that our new products
bring to GCP.

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:05.690
However, any hardware is
only as good as the software

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:07.130
that runs on top of it.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:10.340
So in addition to providing
the features in our hardware,

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:13.130
we have been heavily focused
on software optimizations

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:15.650
to leverage all the
features in our hardware.

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:18.710
We have been engaged very
closely over the last few years

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:20.480
with different
framework developers

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:24.770
to integrate our libraries and
upstream our optimizations.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.960
Here's an example
of our optimization

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:29.420
work over the course
of one year and a half.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:34.820
From July 2017, which is when we
launched Skylake CPUs publicly,

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:38.780
there has been about 5.4x
improvement in inference

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:41.750
performance, solely from
software optimizations.

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:45.860
This is a comparison on
the same Intel hardware

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:48.890
and can speak to the power
of our optimizations.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:52.820
From now till next year, when
our next generation Xeon CPUs

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:54.770
will be available
publicly, we're

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:57.620
expecting inference
performance to roughly double,

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:59.300
due to the new
hardware acceleration

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:02.390
instructions, implementation,
and the additional performance

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:04.610
optimizations.

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:07.680
This chart is based on
ResNet 50 performance.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.130
Obviously for a less
compute-bound workload,

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:12.320
where there are other
bottlenecks in the hardware,

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.750
the results will be less
pronounced but still

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:16.052
very significant.

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:22.600
Now, let's focus on
TensorFlow for a bit

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:26.020
and talk briefly about the
framework optimizations.

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:28.810
We spoke earlier about our
optimized deep neural network

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:29.600
libraries.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:33.790
MKL-DNN, as I mentioned, is our
open source Math Kernel Library

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:37.390
for deep neural networks
and the main building block

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:40.740
of our framework optimizations.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:44.820
MKL-DNN is highly optimized
using sometimes very low level,

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.170
as low as assembly code.

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:50.880
And the API has been developed
with feedback and interactions

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.070
with major framework owners.

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:56.010
The optimizations are
done at multiple layers.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:58.650
First, all the primitives,
such as convolution,

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:01.350
matrix multiplication,
and batch normalization

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:04.620
are rewritten to take advantage
of the latest wide vector

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:06.450
instructions.

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:08.910
Next, the different
layers are parallelized

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:12.180
to allow for efficient use
of all the available cores

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:13.560
in the hardware.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:16.650
And lastly, balanced
use of pre-fetching,

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:19.440
cache blocking techniques,
and data formats

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:22.170
that promote spatial
and temporal locality

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:25.620
are used to make data more
available when the execution

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:28.520
units need it.

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:31.360
In addition to using
MKL-DNN primitives,

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:34.720
we use other techniques to
integrate into TensorFlow.

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.540
There are things that we do
like layout optimization,

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:41.020
replacing default TensorFlow
operations, fuse operations,

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:43.120
propagating intermediate states.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:47.430
And we have our custom
CPU pool allocator,

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:51.060
which basically helps with
avoiding costly page misses

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:54.420
and page clears.

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.300
We have been working with
Google on these optimizations

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.090
for two-plus years and
continue to collaborate

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:03.000
to realize the gains from the
new instructions and framework

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:03.750
performance.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.850
Now, in addition to TensorFlow
and other deep learning

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:13.740
frameworks, we have
been contributing

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:16.980
our optimized
libraries to Python.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.980
We have accelerated NumPy,
SciPy, and SciKit-Learn

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:24.510
with Intel MKL.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:27.630
Our optimizations are available
through conda and Anaconda

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:30.480
Cloud for general public
use and are upstreamed back

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:32.149
to main Python trunk.

00:13:32.149 --> 00:13:33.690
If you're interested
to find out more

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:36.570
on Intel distribution of
Python and some performance

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:38.460
proof [INAUDIBLE],, we
have a good write-up

