00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:49.980
and accessing the
optimized images.

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.260
To start off in Google
Compute Engine, when you're

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:56.130
creating an instance, go to
the CPU platform pull-down menu

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.110
and select Intel
Skylake or later

00:16:58.110 --> 00:17:00.640
to guarantee that
your application will

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:04.140
leverage the latest
hardware enhancements.

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:06.720
We talked earlier about
the advantages of Skylake

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:09.480
over a previous generation CPU.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.940
And I'm glad to mention that
in terms of availability,

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:15.869
Skylake is now available in
16 out of 17 GCP regions.

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:22.650
If you're not using
the console, you

00:17:22.650 --> 00:17:26.640
can use the min-cpu-platform
flag in Cloud Shell

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:28.349
to select your CPU.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:31.560
In any of the 16 zones
with Skylake availability,

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:33.630
you can set the flag
to Intel Skylake

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:36.780
when you create your VM to
guarantee running on Skylake.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:44.570
As we discussed earlier, the
main premise of the VM images

00:17:44.570 --> 00:17:47.390
is to make Intel
optimizations and tools easily

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:50.010
available to GCP developers.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:53.040
One of the ways for accessing
the VM images with Intel

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:55.680
optimized TensorFlow
or Intel optimized

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:59.820
Python is to use Google
Cloud Marketplace.

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:02.520
In your Google Cloud
console environment,

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:06.330
click on Marketplace and look
for deep learning VM images.

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:08.370
With one click, you
will be able to deploy

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:09.840
these images on your VM.

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:17.360
You can also access
these images on GCE.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:19.910
If you're looking for more
control when you're setting up

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:23.480
your VM, deploy these
optimized OS images

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:25.940
by changing the boot
disk and selecting one

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:28.040
of the Intel optimized images.

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:30.080
To take full advantage
of the optimizations

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:31.790
on the latest
architecture, make sure

00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:35.030
that you select your CPU
platform as Intel Skylake

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:36.560
or later while doing this.

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:42.150
And if you're using Google
call command line interface,

00:18:42.150 --> 00:18:44.730
you can launch the VM images
using the following command

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:45.630
line options.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:49.200
Set the image family variable to
one of the Intel-specific image

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:52.330
types at the time you are
creating your instance.

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:54.600
You can also set the
min-cpu-platform variable

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:58.100
to the latest
Intel CPU, Skylake.

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:08.660
Now I'd like to talk to
you about our toolkit.

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:10.100
So we talked about
the libraries.

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:12.980
We talked about different
layers of software stack.

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:17.180
And we focused on the libraries
and frameworks so far.

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:20.120
And I'd like to talk to you a
little bit about our toolkit

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:24.330
and what helps you develop
for Intel hardware.

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:26.820
So our latest project
with Google Cloud

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:28.720
has been on Kubeflow pipeline.

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:30.300
And we've been
working on integrating

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:34.350
part of our development toolkit
into the Kubeflow pipeline.

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:37.440
Earlier we talked a little
bit about our software toolkit

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:40.260
with a common API across
different Intel hardware.

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:42.570
This software toolkit
is known as OpenVino,

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:45.450
which stands for
open visual inference

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:48.250
and neural net optimization.

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:52.770
OpenVino has three
main components.

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:57.970
First is a deep learning
deployment kit, or DLDT.

00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:03.080
The second one is
an OpenCV and Media

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:07.480
SDK used for traditional
vision and video processing.

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:10.840
And third is a collection
of pre-trained models

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:12.370
and tutorials.

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:17.170
In summary, OpenVino provides
a unified programming model

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:22.440
across hardware, is optimized
for performance and memory

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:29.680
footprint, is extensible
for custom workloads,

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:33.070
provides broad operating
system support,

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:36.460
is self-contained for a simple
out-of-the-box experience,

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:39.550
and is open-source for the
community to contribute

00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:41.830
and enhance.

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:45.190
The components that
we will focus on today

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:49.930
are basically the DLDT, which
offers a model optimizer

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:53.920
and an inference engine
with unified APIs

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:55.420
across different Intel hardware.

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:02.270
So Intel and Google
are partnering

00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:05.480
to bring the performance
and ease of use of DLDT

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:10.280
to GCP developers by adding
DLDT containers to the Kubeflow

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:11.910
pipeline.

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:14.805
Suppose you're a data scientist,
creating an end-to-end solution

00:21:14.805 --> 00:21:17.430
based on some medical images.

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:21.780
Your work can be done through
multiple different containers.

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.750
The data ingestion,
data transformation,

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:29.410
and data validation containers
facilitate reading the data,

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:31.800
transforming the data
to the expected format,

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:36.990
and validating the data as
usable and properly labeled.

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:39.900
This data is passed to
the trainer container.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:41.940
The output is a
trained model that

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:44.130
is passed to the model
validation container

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:48.390
to ensure the model is ready
for deployment or serving.

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:53.810
The trained model is then used
for serving by your end party.

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:56.840
The way OpenVino is
integrated into this flow

00:21:56.840 --> 00:22:03.420
is that the trained model is
fed to the DLDT model optimizer.

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:08.250
The model optimizer
generates optimized IR.

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:12.030
And then the optimized IR
is fed into the serving

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:16.530
with DLDT container basically
using the inference engine

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:19.870
to run this as efficiently as
possible on the Intel hardware

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:20.370
backend.

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:30.180
So here we have some examples of
integrating Kubeflow pipelines

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:32.650
with OpenVino containers.

